tools.doc:
Run a command with `sh -c` in the workspace directory and hand back its merged standard output and standard error.

A non-zero exit is **not** a failure of this call: the output and the code both come back so your program can branch on them. Checking whether a build or a test run passed is the single most common thing a program does, so it has to work inside an expression:

```csharp
var built = system.Shell("cargo build --release");
if (built.ExitCode != 0)
{
    view.OpenText("build", built.Output);
}
```

Only a failure to launch the process, or the timeout killing it, throws.

Returns: what the process wrote and how it exited.

Throws `ToolException`: `ToolErrorCode.LimitExceeded` when the timeout killed it, and `ToolErrorCode.IOError` when the process could not be started at all.

types.doc:
What a child agent is asked to do: either a brief you write here, or a board issue whose scope and completion criteria become the brief.

It is exactly one of the two, and that is the whole reason it is a type rather than two nullable arguments — "neither" and "both" are not requests gg can carry out, and neither of them compiles.

```csharp
agents.SpawnSubagent("reviewer", Brief.Issue("AUTH-3"));
agents.SpawnSubagent("worker", Brief.Prompt("port the fixtures to the new schema"));
```

What a read returned — either `TextFile` or `ImageFile`, and never both.

A picture is a different kind of thing from text, so it is a different case: a program that treated an image as text is caught by the type system here rather than silently writing an empty string somewhere. The format is detected from the file's bytes, never from its extension.

Narrow it the way you narrow any closed hierarchy in C#:

```csharp
if (fs.ReadFile("logo.png") is TextFile text)
{
    view.OpenText("logo", text.Contents);
}
else if (fs.ReadFile("logo.png") is ImageFile picture)
{
    view.OpenText("logo", $"{picture.Label}, {picture.Bytes} bytes");
}
```

A three-way edit of an optional text field: leave it, empty it, or replace it.

It exists so that "clear the description" and "set the description to the empty string" stop being the same request. The default is `TextEdit.Keep`, so an update that does not mention a description does not touch it.

```csharp
tasks.UpdateTask("build", description: TextEdit.Set("cargo build --release"));
tasks.UpdateTask("build", description: TextEdit.Clear);
```

The exception every gg call throws when it fails.

A failure is thrown rather than returned because C# is an exception language: the common path reads as a straight line, and the calls you expect to fail are the ones you wrap.

```csharp
try
{
    var notes = fs.ReadTextFile("NOTES.md");
    view.OpenText("notes", notes);
}
catch (ToolException failure) when (failure.Code == ToolErrorCode.NotFound)
{
    view.OpenText("notes", "there are no notes yet");
}
```

Let the ones you did not expect escape: gg reports which call failed, with the managed stack, and the turn is recoverable.
